# Documentation Structure & DRY Rules

> **Status:** Normative (Mandatory) · **Scope:** All Contributors & AI Agents

This document dictates the architectural boundaries of all documentation files in this repository.
To prevent documentation drift and maintain the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle,
you **MUST** adhere to these boundaries.

## 1. Central Documents (Root Level)

| File | Audience | Purpose | Boundary |
|---|---|---|---|
| `README.md` | 👤 Humans | Project overview, quickstart, structure | **Never** contains AI rules or internal conventions |
| `AGENTS.md` | 🤖 AI Agents | Single source of truth for AI constraints | **Never** duplicates README content |
{% if include_vision_roadmap %}| `ROADMAP.md` | 👤 + 🤖 | The **When** — milestones, versioning, timing | Does **not** describe architecture |
| `VISION.md` | 👤 + 🤖 | The **Why** — architecture ideals, endgame goals | Does **not** track release versions |
{% endif %}
## 2. Developer Guidelines (`docs/development/`)

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `commit.md` | Conventional Commits standard — **binding** for all contributors and agents |

**Rule:** Development docs describe *process* (how to commit, how to test, how to release).
They must **never** paraphrase source code or duplicate information available in `pyproject.toml` or `justfile`.

## 2a. Overlay Documentation (`docs/<overlay>/`)

If an overlay template is applied (expert report, thesis), it owns exactly one
directory under `docs/` — e.g. `docs/gutachten/` or `docs/thesis/` — holding its
binding domain rules and its own `AGENTS.md`. Those files are **maintained upstream**
in the overlay repo and pulled in via `copier update`; edit them there, not here.
Nothing outside that directory may duplicate their content.

## 3. Source Code (`src/`)

The code is the **Single Source of Truth** for implementation details.
Documentation must **never** paraphrase what the code already expresses (e.g., function signatures,
config keys, data schemas). Instead, link to the code or refer to the module by name.

## 4. Scripts (`scripts/`)

Lifecycle and build scripts called by `justfile` targets.
Each script must have a docstring explaining its purpose and which `just` command calls it.

## 5. AI & Automation Tooling

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `.agent/workflows/*.md` | Workflow definitions for autonomous AI agents |
| `AGENTS.md` | Entry point — agents read this first |

**Rule:** Workflow files must **never** write persistent files to the repository.
Audit reports and plans stay in temporary artifacts or chat output.

## 6. General DRY Rules

1. **No content duplication** — link instead of copying.
2. **Code is SoT** — documentation describes *why* and *how to use*, never paraphrases *what the code does*.
3. **Scrap notes** — temporary ideas or obsolete notes go to gitignored directories (`.tmp/`, `_workspace/`). Do not bloat the repository with dead notes.
4. **One owner per topic** — every piece of information has exactly one canonical location. If two files describe the same thing, one must become a link-stub.
